## 75. Final Summary

Instagram is not one application server connected to one database.

It is a collection of specialized distributed systems.

### Upload

- Resumable chunks.
- Upload sessions.
- Idempotent retries.
- Final integrity validation.
- Dedicated upload servers.
- Temporary storage.

### Processing

- Durable queues.
- Scalable workers.
- Deterministic derivatives.
- Metadata handling.
- Content moderation.
- Retry support.

### Storage

- Object storage.
- Lifecycle tiers.
- Cross-region replication.
- CDN delivery.
- Adaptive media variants.
- Distributed deletion.

### Feed

- Push regular-user posts.
- Pull celebrity posts.
- Merge both sources.
- Remove unavailable content.
- Rank candidates.
- Paginate using cursors.
- Degrade gracefully.

### Database

- Query-first Cassandra modeling.
- Denormalized tables.
- Bounded partitions.
- Time buckets.
- Tunable consistency.
- Monitoring and disaster recovery.

The central architectural lesson is separation:

- Separate media bytes from metadata.
- Separate upload from processing.
- Separate regular-user fan-out from celebrity delivery.
- Separate durable candidate generation from optional ranking.
- Separate CDN media delivery from application APIs.
- Use stronger consistency where privacy and trust require it.
- Use controlled eventual consistency where small delays are acceptable.
- Ensure optional features can fail without destroying the core system.
